{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "document_id": "ch-system-aboutdebug",
  "title": "About Debugging Symbols",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Most programs and libraries are, by default, compiled with debugging symbols included (with"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "text": "gcc"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "'s"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "text": "-g"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "option). This means that when debugging a program or library that was compiled with debugging information, the debugger can provide not only memory addresses, but also the names of the routines and variables."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The inclusion of these debugging symbols enlarges a program or library significantly. Here are two examples of the amount of space these symbols occupy:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A"
              },
              {
                "type": "code",
                "text": "bash"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "binary with debugging symbols: 1200 KB"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A"
              },
              {
                "type": "code",
                "text": "bash"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "binary without debugging symbols: 480 KB (60% smaller)"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Glibc and GCC files ("
              },
              {
                "type": "code",
                "text": "/lib"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "and"
              },
              {
                "type": "code",
                "text": "/usr/lib"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ") with debugging symbols: 87 MB"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Glibc and GCC files without debugging symbols: 16 MB (82% smaller)"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Sizes will vary depending on which compiler and C library were used, but a program that has been stripped of debugging symbols is usually some 50% to 80% smaller than its unstripped counterpart. Because most users will never use a debugger on their system software, a lot of disk space can be regained by removing these symbols. The next section shows how to strip all debugging symbols from the programs and libraries."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
